WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.020
Nature loves balance.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.900
Ever sprinkle salt on a piece of eggplant?

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.320
Over time, the eggplant starts to sweat out water.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.400
This happens because there is a different ratio

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.880
of salt to water on the surface versus

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.460
inside the eggplant, so water seeps out

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.040
onto the surface to try and equalize how salty

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.739
the water is inside and out.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.460
This balancing act is called osmosis.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.020
It happens throughout nature –

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.040
even in massive bodies of water –

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.660
like where rivers meet the sea.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.660
Scientists have been trying to harness the power

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.280
of this balancing act --called blue energy

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.220
by sticking thin membranes in between

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.860
sea water and fresh water.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.840
The holes in the membranes are too small

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.960
to allow water to move through, like in the eggplant,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.540
so instead, the charged particles from the salt move.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.160
This flow of charged particles can be

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.880
harnessed to create electricity.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.080
In order for the charges to continue flowing,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.020
the membrane needs to be selective—

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.660
meaning it should only let

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.020
one type of ion through.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.620
Since manufacturing the membranes

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.500
relies on nanotechnology,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.200
it is often expensive to build and maintain them,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.180
and the system can lose efficiency over time.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.180
It’s a challenge to scale up these systems

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.100
to produce enough power to make them worth the cost.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.120
Now, scientists have built a 3-d Janus membrane

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.600
named after the mythical god with two faces.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.500
The Janus membrane has different properties

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.280
on each side, which makes it a good gatekeeper

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.780
for encouraging a steady flow of charged particles

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.820
from one side to the other.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.680
The membrane can be tuned to have different

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.640
sized pores and accept different kinds of

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.020
charged particles.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.740
The researchers measured how efficient this process

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.660
was at converting chemical energy to electrical energy.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.080
At about 35.7 percent it’s better than a solar cell

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.080
but not as good as a wind turbine.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.020
The team hopes that Janus membranes can be

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.520
used to create more efficient and less expensive

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.430
blue energy generators.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.560
These systems can be deployed to generate power

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.760
in coastal communities that don’t have large enough

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.020
waterfalls or strong enough currents

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.960
for other forms of water power -

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.220
like underwater turbines.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.560
In order to find new forms of renewable energy

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.180
and limit carbon emissions,

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:13.060
researchers are looking in unexpected places -

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.800
even tapping into salty solutions.

